The all-particle cosmic-ray energy spectrum (differential flux of cosmic-ray particles arriving at Earth as a function of kinetic energy E) shows three distinct spectral breaks in the energy range 10^15 eV ≤ E ≤ 10^18 eV. In order of increasing energy these are called:
  (i) the "knee", a steepening of the spectrum near E ≈ 3 × 10^15 eV;
  (ii) the "second knee" (also called the "iron knee"), a further steepening near E ≈ 10^17 eV;
  (iii) the "ankle", a hardening of the spectrum near E ≈ 5 × 10^18 eV.
At sufficiently low energies cosmic rays are believed to be produced by sources within the Milky Way ("Galactic" cosmic rays), while at the highest energies they are believed to originate outside the Galaxy ("extragalactic" cosmic rays). Candidate Galactic accelerators include supernova remnants (SNRs), whose maximum achievable particle energy E_max is set by the acceleration mechanism (e.g. diffusive shock acceleration) and the source environment. Spectral features can in principle arise either from such source-imposed maximum energies or from changes in cosmic-ray propagation through the Galaxy (e.g. energy-dependent diffusion or escape from the Galactic magnetic field).

Determine:
  (a) the physical mechanism responsible for each of the knee, second knee/iron knee, and ankle features — in particular, decide whether the knee is produced by an energy-dependent change in Galactic propagation, by the maximum energy attainable by Galactic sources such as SNRs (and whether SNRs can in fact accelerate protons up to ≈ 3 PeV), or by some other mechanism;
  (b) at which of these three features the transition from a predominantly Galactic to a predominantly extragalactic component of the cosmic-ray flux begins.

